# =====================================================================
# kernel.clausal -- eu/aml/amlr_beneficial_ownership_threshold SHARED
# FLOOR (the determination-slices DAG root; formerly schema.clausal).
# EMPTY BY MEASUREMENT, not by omission.
#
# WHY THERE IS NOTHING TO PUT HERE, by the recipe's decision procedure:
#
#  1. Does this domain MINT its own compound result constructor? NO. The
#     verdict shape is the KIT's: assess_amlr_bo/3 wraps formalize_lib's
#     assessment/3 over item/3 checklist rows, the requirement status
#     terms are formalize_lib's met / unmet(REASON) / unknown(KEYS) built
#     by status_gte, and the citation wrapper cite/1 belongs to
#     citations.clausal (shared, out of scope). The domain's own answer
#     channel is a BARE ATOM in amlr_bo_status/2's and amlr_bo_decision/2's
#     second argument -- eligible / ineligible / indeterminate -- plus an
#     exact rational in basis points. There is no domain-owned X_verdict/N
#     or X_result/N term and no element constructor for a consumer to
#     pattern-match by name, so the unconditional result-shape-constructor
#     category is EMPTY here.
#
#     Rule 3 (input-constructor closure) has no instance either: every
#     argument the public predicates are queried with -- by
#     eval/score_harness.py, by the three tests/ files, and by the
#     flip_scan closures in queries.clausal -- is a plain list of integer
#     basis points, a native dict profile, a string subject id, or an
#     integer. eval/score_harness.py builds its profile term with
#     auto.eval_harness.profile_terms, whose KEYS are looked up by name off
#     the loaded rulebase (__init__.clausal) -- an ATOM lookup, which the
#     engine lowers by identity, not a compound constructor.
#
#  2. Do any VALUE ATOMS clear the strict ">= 2 SLICES" bar? NO -- this
#     domain has exactly ONE determination slice
#     (beneficial_ownership.clausal, the Art 52(1) ownership-interest
#     test), so no name can be shared between two slices. The six atoms
#     schema.clausal used to hold (eligible, ineligible, indeterminate,
#     effective_holding_below_25_percent_threshold,
#     required_ownership_threshold, ownership_chains) are spelled by the
#     slice and by the TOP-LAYER consumers queries.clausal / fixtures.clausal;
#     top-layer consumers do not count toward the kernel bar, and they
#     import those names from the owning slice instead.
#
# Kept as a file (rather than omitted) per the recipe's target layout, and
# because `auto/layout.py:is_sliced` reads `kernel.clausal`'s PRESENCE as
# THE marker that this tree is a determination-slice package -- without it
# `dag_order` falls back to the layer literal (schema/constants/computation/
# decision/...), none of which exists here any more, and the package facade
# pkginit generates would silently lose the whole determination.
# =====================================================================

-module(kernel, [])
-double_quotes(chars)

-strict_atoms
